s buildup that  provided some insight into why Allison  saw things the way she did and this  could undermine a lot of that this isn't  something from the book The Prophecy  itself isn't in there at all and it  feels like this gives her a clear-cut  reason to block runner's claim at the  same time it's a little early to assign  the simplest explanation of what they  might be trying to do it did give them  the chance to Swerve once and have  everybody come together only for that to  fall apart and admittedly part of my  reaction comes from this being different  than what I was expecting and that's  where things get interesting because  here is the thing we know what the  historical count says we know what the  Fire and Blood history says about the  reign of viserys Targaryen and his death  and now we're getting a lot more details  as to what actually happened and so I  think that it makes sense that a show  based on a book that relies on  unreliable accounts might raise the  question of how reliable that  information is can you believe what you  read when it comes to history should you  because it all comes through someone  else after the fact and I think you  should at least keep in mind that it's  probably more complicated than it  appears is that what house of the dragon  is trying to show us for instance in  this case the history is written by men  in a society that is pretty misogynistic  and therefore women are likely to be  painted in an unfavorable light it's  kind of funny because there's a lot of  discourse around which side is right  with fans picking aside like they're  watching sports I've never really had a  clear favorite because it just seems  like trying to decide which group is a  little less wrong when the system is  there to dictate that they have to fight  in the first place it's all entertaining  but none of it seemed really all that  cool to me both sides are capable of  good and bad but the clear thing is that  no one is better off because of t